-- A refined `List` contract COMPOSES across a call boundary: a function that
-- requires `{List(Int) | len(_) > 0}` may pass that very parameter on to
-- another function with the identical requirement, and the callee's
-- obligation is PROVED using the caller's own declared contract alone -- no
-- runtime guard needed at either call site.
--
-- Before 2026-07-29 a `List`/ADT refinement was not admitted to the refined
-- scope channel AT ALL: `refined_scope_ty` accepted only Int, String and
-- record parameters, so `outer`'s promise about `ys` produced no scope entry
-- anywhere -- there was no fact in `scope_facts` or in any other channel for
-- the compositional check to miss. `check_call`'s call-site resolver simply
-- had nothing to consult, and `len$ys` was an unconstrained integer. The
-- result was silent: `inner(ys)` inside `outer`'s body was SKIPPED rather than proved,
-- while `outer(ys)` from `main` still proved fine on its own -- so the
-- program looked identical (exit 0) whether the inner call composed or was
-- simply ignored.
--
-- That is exactly why this file is bracketed by the rejecting
-- `reject/t129_refine_weaker_contract_composition_still_fails`: it shows the
-- SAME two-hop shape does NOT let a weaker caller contract launder a
-- stronger callee requirement, so exit 0 here cannot be explained away as
-- "the checker accepts everything it cannot disprove". Composition only
-- fires when the caller's own promise already entails the callee's.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "Path Sensitivity" and the `len`
-- composition tests in `test/test_refinecheck.ml`'s `compose` suite.
mod T128 do

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : Int do
    0
  end

  fn outer(ys : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : Int do
    inner(ys)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    outer([1])
  end

end
